  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What are things first time home buyers should know?",
      "answer": "First-time buyers should establish a firm budget and build emergency savings before shopping. Securing a mortgage pre-approval confirms your borrowing limits before making an offer. Include financing, appraisal, and inspection contingencies in your purchase agreement to protect your earnest money. Read our complete [first-time home buyer guide](/guides/first-time-home-buyer-guide/) for detailed step-by-step guidance."
    },
    {
      "question": "What should I look for when buying a house?",
      "answer": "Look for visible water stains, aging roof coverings, and outdated electrical panels during initial property showings. Check the age and condition of heating and cooling equipment as well as property tax histories. For managed communities, examine association rules and monthly assessment fees. Use our detailed [home inspection checklist](/checklists/home-inspection-checklist/) to guide your physical review."
    },
    {
      "question": "What are the steps in the home buying process?",
      "answer": "The process moves across six distinct milestones starting with budgeting and mortgage pre-approval. You then tour houses, submit an offer with protective contingencies, and complete professional property inspections. The final stretch involves securing homeowners insurance, completing an appraisal, and finalizing loan documents before closing. A seventh follow-up phase covers safety tasks during your first week in the house."
    },
    {
      "question": "When do I get the Closing Disclosure?",
      "answer": "Your mortgage lender must provide the Closing Disclosure at least three business days before your scheduled closing date. This federal requirement gives you adequate time to compare final loan charges against your original Loan Estimate. Review every fee, interest rate, and required cash-to-close figure during this three-day period."
    },
    {
      "question": "How does the checklist change for a condo?",
      "answer": "Selecting a condo adds tasks for reviewing condominium association financial reserves, monthly HOA dues, and common element rules. If you use FHA or VA financing, the checklist adds a step to confirm project-level approval for the development. It also removes external maintenance items that the master association handles, such as private septic tanks or standalone roof framing."
    },
    {
      "question": "Do I need flood insurance?",
      "answer": "Flood insurance is required by lenders on most mortgages when the home sits in a high-risk flood hazard area. Standard homeowners policies generally exclude damage caused by external flooding and rising surface water. You can look up any property address on the official [FEMA Flood Map Service Center](https://msc.fema.gov/portal/home) to identify its flood zone."
    },
    {
      "question": "What changes on the checklist if I pay cash?",
      "answer": "Paying cash removes the mortgage pre-approval milestone from your task list entirely. It also drops lender-required items like Loan Estimates, lender appraisal mandates, escrow reserves, and mandatory mortgage insurance. Property inspections, title searches, municipal transfer documents, and final walkthrough checks remain on your list."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I save this checklist as a PDF?",
      "answer": "Yes, you can generate a clean PDF directly from this page without signing up. Click the Print or save as PDF button located near the bottom of the tool. When your browser print dialog opens, set the printer destination to Save as PDF and save the worksheet to your device."
